meta {
  name: ClarificationNonWhitelisted
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/web/ai-search
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Auth-Token: {{authToken}}
  Content-Type: application/json
  Accept: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "prompt": "hoeveel dingen zijn er"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.resultType: eq clarification
  res.body.entity: eq null
  res.body.intent: eq null
  res.body.items: eq []
}

docs {
  Vague/non-selective prompt -> resultType "clarification" (still 200 OK,
  not an error - see Backend/README.md "AI Search" section and
  Luna.BusinessLogic.AiSearchBE:ValidateIntentAgainstWhitelist). Also
  covers the "not whitelisted entity/field/operator" case from the design
  contract - any of those produce the same resultType.

  IMPORTANT: this scenario is normally reached via Gemini stage 1
  returning a low-confidence/ambiguous intent for a vague prompt. Without
  GEMINI_API_KEY configured (see Backend/README.md), stage 1 itself fails
  first and this request instead returns 502 - confirmed live in the dev
  environment this feature was built against. Configure GEMINI_API_KEY on
  the PASOE agent to exercise this specific 200/clarification path; until
  then, treat a 502 here as "stage 1 unavailable", not "clarification
  logic is broken" - the clarification logic itself is unit-testable via
  Luna.BusinessLogic.AiSearchBE:ValidateIntentAgainstWhitelist with a
  hand-built intent, independent of a live Gemini call.
}

